# Header field test vectors and RFC test messages of Stalwart mail-parser, https://github.com/stalwartlabs/mail-parser at commit
# 4eba485d9450c757b2f3ec29c44663740cff0afe, copied byte for byte with curl on 2026-09-27, each from the source URL followed by its
# path. The build verifies each file against its SHA-256 and fails on a mismatch, a missing file or an unlisted one
# (project/VendoredFiles.scala).
#
# Each vector is {"header": <a field body after the name and colon, with its line end>, "expected": <Stalwart's reading>}: another
# parser's reading, compared with the module's, never taken as truth. Every vector was read before vendoring; they are synthetic
# (test phrases, RFC 2047 and RFC 5322 examples, example domains). One subject names a reservation number and no person.
# content_type.json: 103 Content-Type and Content-Disposition field bodies, read whole before vendoring: RFC 2045, 2046, 2183 and
# 2231 examples, parser edge cases, and synthetic file names. Vector 101 is a file name taken from an order confirmation with the
# names already replaced by X ("Auftragsbestatigung Xxxxx Xxxxxx XXXXXXXXXX.PDF"); it names no person and is kept.
# date.json: 40 Date field bodies, read whole: RFC 5322 examples and synthetic dates; vector 28 is the date part of a Received
# trace from a real message (a Google SMTP id, no person, no address), kept.
# id.json: 11 message-id field bodies, read whole: RFC 5322 examples and synthetic ids.
# rfc-NNN.eml: the ten messages of resources/eml/rfc/NNN.eml, renamed with the rfc- prefix, read whole: RFC 2046, 2049 and 8621
# examples and synthetic messages. rfc-002.eml is the well-known fabricated "Map of Argentina" test message, a parody naming public
# officials with deliberate misspellings (fi1ename, mai1host); it is not correspondence and is kept. Stalwart's JSON readings of
# them (NNN.json, NNN.crlf.json) are not vendored: the tests compare with mime4j instead.
# Excluded: resources/address.json. Its vectors 60 and 61 are names of real people taken from the Enron mail corpus, which is real
# correspondence; a vendored file is never edited, so the whole file is left out.
#
# The build reads no subdirectory, so LICENSES/Apache-2.0.txt and LICENSES/MIT.txt are vendored as LICENSE-Apache-2.0.txt and
# LICENSE-MIT.txt.
#
# Licence: Cargo.toml says license = "Apache-2.0 OR MIT", with both texts beside this file; README.md: "Copyright (C) 2020,
# Stalwart Labs LLC". The repository has no NOTICE file. The files are used under the Apache-2.0 election, every condition of which
# the files here meet; upstream's MIT text carries no copyright line of its own for MIT's notice condition to refer to.
